          title: "The High Priestly Prayer",
          body: "The long farewell is over; now Jesus stops speaking *to* the disciples and speaks *to the Father*, and they overhear it. Since the fourth century this chapter has been called the **High Priestly Prayer** — for here, on the eve of the sacrifice, the great High Priest prays for the people he is about to die for, consecrating both himself and them (v. 19). It moves in three widening circles: he prays first **for himself** (vv. 1–5), that the Father would glorify him through the cross; then **for his disciples** (vv. 6–19), that they be kept, sanctified, and one; and finally **for all who will believe** through their word (vv. 20–26) — which means it is a prayer for *us,* named by Jesus across two thousand years before we were born. The keynote is struck at once: **“Father, the hour has come.”** The hour dreaded and awaited through the whole Gospel is here, and he greets it not with fear but with a request — *glorify your Son* — because he knows that the cross is the throne, and that the way down into death is the way up into glory. To hear this prayer is to be let into the inner room of the Trinity’s love on the night it opened itself to the world.",
