          title: "“This is eternal life — that they know you”",
          body: "It is the closest thing in the New Testament to a definition of eternal life, and it is startling in what it does *not* say. Jesus does not define eternal life as endless duration, or as a place, or as a reward for the good. He defines it as a *relationship:* **“that they know you, the only true God, and Jesus Christ whom you have sent.”** Eternal life is not first something that begins when we die; it is something that begins the moment we come to *know* God — and the word for knowing here is not intellectual acquaintance but the deep, personal knowing of covenant and love, the way a husband knows a wife. So eternal life is *God himself,* known and enjoyed; heaven is not the reward *for* knowing him but the unhindered fullness *of* knowing him. This quietly overturns every merely religious hope. The goal is not immortality for its own sake — an endless existence could be a horror. The goal is *communion:* to know, and be known by, the one true God, through the one he sent. And note that the Son is placed inside the definition alongside the Father — to know eternal life is to know *both,* for the Father is known only through the Son (14:6). Whoever has that knowing has already, now, the life that death cannot end.",
